h2. What happened

{{CREATE TABLE LIKE}} does not preserve the source table's {{DEFAULT COLLATION}}. When a new {{STRING}} column is added to the cloned table, it uses {{UTF8_BINARY}} instead of the source table's {{UTF8_LCASE}} default.

This changes string comparison and grouping results.

h2. How to reproduce

Save the following SQL as {{tmp.sql}}:

{code:sql}
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS t_clone_collation_src;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS t_clone_collation_dst;

CREATE TABLE t_clone_collation_src (s STRING)
USING PARQUET
DEFAULT COLLATION UTF8_LCASE;

CREATE TABLE t_clone_collation_dst LIKE t_clone_collation_src;

ALTER TABLE t_clone_collation_dst ADD COLUMNS (t STRING);

INSERT INTO t_clone_collation_dst VALUES
  ('a', 'a'),
  ('A', 'A');

SELECT t_collation, COUNT(*) AS distinct_t_values
FROM (
  SELECT t, collation(t) AS t_collation
  FROM t_clone_collation_dst
  GROUP BY t, collation(t)
) groups
GROUP BY t_collation
ORDER BY t_collation;

DROP TABLE t_clone_collation_dst;
DROP TABLE t_clone_collation_src;
{code}

Run using {{spark-sql}}:

{code:bash}
spark-sql --master 'local[2]' 
  --conf spark.ui.enabled=false 
  --conf spark.sql.shuffle.partitions=1 
  -f tmp.sql
{code}

h2. Expected result

The cloned table should preserve the source table's {{UTF8_LCASE}} default collation. The newly added column {{t}} should therefore use case-insensitive comparison, grouping {{a}} and {{A}} together.

{code:none}
t_collation               | distinct_t_values
--------------------------+------------------
SYSTEM.BUILTIN.UTF8_LCASE | 1
{code}

h2. Actual result

The newly added column uses {{UTF8_BINARY}}, so {{a}} and {{A}} are treated as distinct values.

{code:none}
t_collation                | distinct_t_values
---------------------------+------------------
SYSTEM.BUILTIN.UTF8_BINARY | 2
{code}

h2. What happened

After changing a partitioned table's root location using {{ALTER TABLE ... SET LOCATION}}, {{ALTER TABLE ... RECOVER PARTITIONS}} can change the location of an already registered partition.

As a result, subsequent queries return different data for the same partition, even though its location was never explicitly changed.

h2. How to reproduce

Save the following SQL as {{tmp.sql}}:

{code:sql}
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS t_set_location_existing;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS t_set_location_new;

CREATE TABLE t_set_location_existing (
  id INT,
  value STRING
)
USING PARQUET
PARTITIONED BY (p STRING)
LOCATION '/tmp/spark_t_set_location_old';

INSERT INTO t_set_location_existing VALUES
  (1, 'old-a', 'a'),
  (2, 'old-b', 'b');

CREATE TABLE t_set_location_new (
  id INT,
  value STRING
)
USING PARQUET
PARTITIONED BY (p STRING)
LOCATION '/tmp/spark_t_set_location_new';

INSERT INTO t_set_location_new VALUES (3, 'new-b', 'b');

ALTER TABLE t_set_location_existing RECOVER PARTITIONS;

SELECT id, value, p
FROM t_set_location_existing
ORDER BY id;

ALTER TABLE t_set_location_existing
SET LOCATION '/tmp/spark_t_set_location_new';

SELECT id, value, p
FROM t_set_location_existing
ORDER BY id;

ALTER TABLE t_set_location_existing RECOVER PARTITIONS;

SELECT id, value, p
FROM t_set_location_existing
ORDER BY id;
{code}

Start with clean directories and run:

{code:bash}
rm -rf /tmp/spark_t_set_location_old 
       /tmp/spark_t_set_location_new

spark-sql --master 'local[2]' 
  --conf spark.ui.enabled=false 
  --conf spark.sql.shuffle.partitions=1 
  -f tmp.sql
{code}

h2. Expected result

Changing the table's root location should not change the locations of already registered partitions. The subsequent {{RECOVER PARTITIONS}} should not replace existing partitions.

All three queries should return:

{code:none}
id | value | p
---+-------+---
1  | old-a | a
2  | old-b | b
{code}

h2. Actual result

After the initial {{RECOVER PARTITIONS}}, the query returns:

{code:none}
id | value | p
---+-------+---
1  | old-a | a
2  | old-b | b
{code}

After {{ALTER TABLE ... SET LOCATION}}, the query still returns:

{code:none}
id | value | p
---+-------+---
1  | old-a | a
2  | old-b | b
{code}

However, after running {{RECOVER PARTITIONS}} again, the result changes to:

{code:none}
id | value | p
---+-------+---
1  | old-a | a
3  | new-b | b
{code}

The existing partition {{p=b}} now returns data from the new table root instead of its original location, although no partition-level location change was requested.

h2. What happened

After changing a database's location using {{ALTER DATABASE ... SET LOCATION}}, {{DROP DATABASE ... CASCADE}} removes the database metadata but leaves the data files of an existing managed table at its original location.

h2. How to reproduce

Save the following SQL as {{tmp.sql}}:

{code:sql}
CREATE DATABASE t_cascade_location
LOCATION '/tmp/spark_t_cascade_location_20261002/old';

CREATE TABLE t_cascade_location.t (
  id INT,
  label STRING
)
USING PARQUET;

INSERT INTO t_cascade_location.t VALUES (7, 'kept');

DESCRIBE EXTENDED t_cascade_location.t;

ALTER DATABASE t_cascade_location
SET LOCATION '/tmp/spark_t_cascade_location_20261002/new';

DROP DATABASE t_cascade_location CASCADE;

SHOW DATABASES LIKE 't_cascade_location';
{code}

Use a fresh base directory or change the path suffix before running the SQL.

Run with the in-memory session catalog:

{code:bash}
spark-sql --master 'local[2]' 
  --conf spark.ui.enabled=false 
  --conf spark.sql.catalogImplementation=in-memory 
  -f tmp.sql
{code}

Then check the original managed-table location:

{code:bash}
find /tmp/spark_t_cascade_location_20261002/old/t 
  -type f -name '*.parquet' -print
{code}

h2. Expected result

{{DROP DATABASE ... CASCADE}} should remove the database and its managed table, including the table's data at its original location.

{{SHOW DATABASES LIKE 't_cascade_location'}} should return no rows.

The filesystem check should return no Parquet files:

{code:none}
(no output)
{code}

h2. Actual result

The database is removed from the catalog, but the original managed-table data remains.

{{SHOW DATABASES LIKE 't_cascade_location'}} returns no rows.

The filesystem check still returns a Parquet file:

{code:none}
/tmp/spark_t_cascade_location_20261002/old/t/part-....snappy.parquet
{code}

The managed table's data files remain on disk after {{DROP DATABASE ... CASCADE}} completes successfully.

h2. What happened

{{ALTER TABLE ADD IF NOT EXISTS PARTITION}} changes the location of an existing partition when a different {{LOCATION}} is specified. As a result, subsequent queries return data from the new location instead of the original partition data.
h2. How to reproduce

Run the following SQL using {{{}spark-sql{}}}:
{code:sql}
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS t_add_partition_if_not_exists;
DROP TABLE IF EXISTS t_add_partition_if_not_exists_alternate;

CREATE TABLE t_add_partition_if_not_exists (
  id INT,
  value STRING
)
USING PARQUET
PARTITIONED BY (p STRING)
LOCATION '/tmp/spark_t_add_partition_if_not_exists_old';

INSERT INTO t_add_partition_if_not_exists VALUES (1, 'old', 'a');

CREATE TABLE t_add_partition_if_not_exists_alternate (
  id INT,
  value STRING
)
USING PARQUET
PARTITIONED BY (p STRING)
LOCATION '/tmp/spark_t_add_partition_if_not_exists_alternate';

INSERT INTO t_add_partition_if_not_exists_alternate VALUES (2, 'alternate', 'a');
DROP TABLE t_add_partition_if_not_exists_alternate;

SELECT id, value, p
FROM t_add_partition_if_not_exists
ORDER BY id;

ALTER TABLE t_add_partition_if_not_exists
  ADD IF NOT EXISTS PARTITION (p = 'a')
  LOCATION '/tmp/spark_t_add_partition_if_not_exists_alternate/p=a';

SHOW PARTITIONS t_add_partition_if_not_exists;

SELECT id, value, p
FROM t_add_partition_if_not_exists
ORDER BY id;
{code}
Save the SQL as {{{}repro.sql{}}}. Start with clean directories and run:
{code:bash}
rm -rf /tmp/spark_t_add_partition_if_not_exists_old 
       /tmp/spark_t_add_partition_if_not_exists_alternate

spark-sql --master 'local[2]' 
  --conf spark.ui.enabled=false 
  -f repro.sql
{code}
h2. Expected result

Since partition {{p=a}} already exists, {{ADD IF NOT EXISTS PARTITION}} should leave its location unchanged.

Both queries should return:
{code}
id | value | p
---+-------+---
1  | old   | a
{code}
h2. Actual result

Before the {{ALTER TABLE}} statement, the query returns:
{code}
id | value | p
---+-------+---
1  | old   | a
{code}
After {{{}ADD IF NOT EXISTS PARTITION{}}}, the query returns:
{code}
id | value     | p
---+-----------+---
2  | alternate | a
{code}
{{SHOW PARTITIONS}} still lists only {{{}p=a{}}}, but the existing partition now reads data from the alternate location.
